{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Session halted on spend headroom — handoff state\n\nThe standing orchestrator loop was disarmed and the session stopped deliberately, on operator decision, with the Bedrock day cap at roughly 9% remaining and 19 hours until the day rolls at 00:00 UTC. No work is in flight. Resume after the roll.\n\n### The operating-model error that caused the halt\n\nPrior session guidance recorded background agents, tracker reads and writes, and orchestrator analysis as **free**, with only `review_plan_start`, `verify_completion_start`, `review_code`, `scan_spec` and Gerrit pushes billable. **That is wrong and it is the single most important correction in this session.**\n\nThe metered figure the spend-cap Lambda acts on is derived from CloudWatch `AWS/Bedrock` token counts, which capture **every** Bedrock call in the account — including the orchestrator session itself and every background agent it dispatches. Measured token counts for the UTC day: 143,268,846 tokens across 4 Anthropic models, essentially all of it agent and orchestrator traffic.\n\nThe evidence that settled it: the review bot finished its run at 03:40 UTC, yet the metered figure continued climbing steeply through 03:56 and 04:26, during a window whose only activity was one read-only investigation agent and orchestrator analysis. A guard that excludes agent dispatch from \"billable\" does not actually stop spending.\n\n**Anyone resuming this loop must treat agent dispatch and its own reasoning as metered.**\n\n### Landed this session\n\n- **2984** merged as `8f7417079ad` (Bedrock spend-cap Terraform restored; 4 files, 2,299 insertions). MCP gate workspace fast-forwarded to it.\n- Tickets closed with signed attestations: `8c79`, `f5ac`, `9b4a`, `0eba`.\n\n### In flight, blocked\n\n- **Change 2988** (`d31375d26a9`, ticket 8806, MCP code workspace refresh on startup): **LLM-Review +1 PASS** with 4 advisory comments, but **Verified -1** on `build-and-test`, pytest `ubuntu-latest, py3.11`. The specific failing assertion was **not** diagnosed — the run log's visible lines were Gerrit-fetch setup noise, not the failure. Diagnosing this is the first task on resume. Scope is 3 files, matching the certified `file_impact`.\n\n### Findings recorded, ready to act on\n\n- **8d27** — not closable. AC1 and AC2 are met; AC3 and AC6 cannot pass because the landed HCL declares 4 variables with no defaults (`bedrock_daily_cap_usd`, `bedrock_warn_threshold_usd`, `bedrock_cap_seed_rates`, `bedrock_cap_unknown_rate_per_1k`) while the drift lane plans with no `-var`, no `-var-file` and no `TF_VAR_*`. The drift lane now fails at exit 1 rather than exit 2, so it asserts nothing about drift at all. **Blocked on an operator decision about where those values live** (SSM data sources vs Actions secrets vs tfvars in the state bucket). AC4, AC5, AC7 remain outstanding and AC4/AC5 are unreadable until a plan evaluates.\n- **3083** — root cause confirmed by observation: `_refresh_rates()` filters Cost Explorer to `SERVICE == \"Amazon Bedrock\"`, but this account bills Claude under per-model `Claude ... (Amazon Bedrock Edition)` services, so no Claude rate is ever loaded. The `us.` inference-profile-prefix hypothesis was tested and **eliminated**. Fallback over-prices by ~22.7x. Token shares correct the ticket's premise: the mispriced model is 8.6% of traffic, while Opus 4.8 at 63% is priced from an unvalidated broad seed. Ready for TDD remediation.\n\n### Owed to the operator, still unanswered\n\n1. Where the four spend-cap values live (blocks 8d27).\n2. The rate-card decisions (now informed by the 3083 findings).\n3. The S2 production-swap NO-GO gating epic `polite-antivirus-bedbug`.\n4. The fate of unpushed local commit `7b92d821718` on the shared main checkout, which may be operator WIP.\n\n### Note on the guard itself\n\nKnowing the metered figure is overstated does not create headroom. The warn threshold and the deny policy fire on the number the Lambda computes, and that deny policy detaches Bedrock from every target role — taking down the review bot and all gates. Fixing 3083 is the remedy; spending against the inflated figure is not."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789793421359713944,"uuid":"f157ffb5-a59b-4f71-b3b7-bde12997defb"}